00:00:37.883 --> 00:00:40.300
Although as I'm saying this,
I'm realizing that the slides

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.540
are not there yet.

00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:44.500
But they will be, so
look forward to that.

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:47.350
So this story starts,
as all great stories

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:49.620
do, with a Shiny app.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:51.400
And this Shiny
app in particular,

00:00:51.400 --> 00:00:53.530
uses the Capital Bikeshare data.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:55.780
So if you don't know what
the Capital Bikeshare--

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:58.525
is the system of docked
bicycles in Washington DC.

00:00:58.525 --> 00:00:59.650
That's what they look like.

00:00:59.650 --> 00:01:00.942
That's what the docs look like.

00:01:00.942 --> 00:01:03.070
You can rent a bike
from a dock and ride it

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:04.938
to a different dock,
and dock it there.

00:01:04.938 --> 00:01:07.480
And so you know, what I'm going
to try and do with this Shiny

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:10.958
app, is predict the number of
bikes, at different stations,

00:01:10.958 --> 00:01:11.750
at different times.

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:15.070
So where's my window?

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:15.890
Here's my window.

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:17.560
So this is what
the app looks like.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:20.230
I've got a map of Washington DC.

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:22.060
I can click on a
particular station.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:25.277
You'll see this is at 21st
and M. And if I scroll down,

00:01:25.277 --> 00:01:26.860
now I can see the
number of bikes that

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:28.135
are available at that station.

00:01:28.135 --> 00:01:30.010
Or predicted to be
available at that station,

00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:31.515
throughout the next 24 hours.

00:01:31.515 --> 00:01:33.640
Important maybe, if I wanted
to go get a bike in DC

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:36.380
at 8PM tonight, because there
are going to be none, at 21st

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:37.410
and M. Too bad.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:44.560
So let's talk a little bit
about how this app works.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:46.960
So the Capital
Bikeshare organization

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:50.560
makes available an API, that
provides real time bike data.

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:53.230
How many bikes at each
station, right now,

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:54.160
available on the API.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.008
And I'm going to import this
using an R Markdown document.

00:01:57.008 --> 00:01:59.050
From there, I'm going to
build an XG Boost model,

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:00.342
to do all my prediction for me.

00:02:00.342 --> 00:02:02.960
I'm going to do that
also in R Markdown.

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:05.780
I'm going to serve this
model with a Plumber API,

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:07.340
and if you're
interested in Plumber--

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:09.507
I'm not going to talk a lot
more about it right now,

00:02:09.507 --> 00:02:11.570
but hang out because like
the rest of this track

00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:12.903
is going to be a lot of Plumber.

00:02:12.903 --> 00:02:14.090
So that'll be awesome.

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:15.650
And then of course,
I'm serving this

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:17.270
to the client app at the end.

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:20.933
And so I had this app, but
I wanted to take it further.

00:02:20.933 --> 00:02:22.100
It just lives on my machine.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:25.430
I don't want it to walk
my computer to people,

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:26.450
to show them my app.

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:28.880
And so I knew where I wanted
to get to, was you know,

00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:33.530
my shiny app is available all
the time to whoever wants it.

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:36.182
The model gets
retrained every day,

00:02:36.182 --> 00:02:38.390
like whether is going to
change, different seasons.

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:42.680
I want to be able to have the
model retraining periodically,

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:44.650
and I need to do this data
import every 20 minutes.

00:02:44.650 --> 00:02:47.750
It's real time data so like
once it's gone, it's gone.

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:49.820
And so I want to get
those real time data,

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:51.830
pull them in, and store
them somewhere so that I

00:02:51.830 --> 00:02:53.830
can do my predictions,
as sort of a time series,

00:02:53.830 --> 00:02:57.420
or panel kind of thing.

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:00.800
So I'm there, but that leaves like
a whole bunch of questions.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:03.045
What should I prioritize?

00:03:03.045 --> 00:03:05.420
While I'm doing this, there
all these things that I could

00:03:05.420 --> 00:03:09.930
want to do, as I'm trying
to sort of deploy this app,

00:03:09.930 --> 00:03:13.170
and add some horsepower.

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:16.880
And these are like hard,
scary, big questions,

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:19.170
and really what they
boil down to is,

00:03:19.170 --> 00:03:21.300
is my deployment
sophisticated enough?

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:24.650
Am I doing it for real, or not?

00:03:24.650 --> 00:03:27.590
Of course, I then
did what I sometimes

00:03:27.590 --> 00:03:29.210
do when I'm
procrastinating, which was

00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:31.160
I started watching Star Wars.

00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:32.850
Just because, you
know, why not do that?

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:35.030
And you all might
know this guy--

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:37.910
green, big ears, talks funny.

00:03:37.910 --> 00:03:39.560
He's the Jedi master
Yoda of course,

00:03:39.560 --> 00:03:41.960
and he says this line in the
middle one of the movies--

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:43.070
"size matters not.

00:03:43.070 --> 00:03:43.637
Look at me.

00:03:43.637 --> 00:03:45.470
Judge me by my size,
do you--" and I thought

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:46.637
this was really appropriate.

00:03:46.637 --> 00:03:50.180
Like, if you just sub in the
word sophistication here,

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:52.460
this is a great way
to think of, why

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:54.465
do we care about
deploying Shiny apps?

00:03:54.465 --> 00:03:56.840
And of course we care, because
we want to increase value.

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:58.100
We want to provide value.

00:03:58.100 --> 00:03:59.600
Sophistication is irrelevant.

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:01.083
Are you providing value?

00:04:01.083 --> 00:04:02.000
Is the right question.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.480
And so how do we deploy
to increase value?

00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:08.260
How was I going to do that?

00:04:08.260 --> 00:04:09.890
There were sort of
three principles

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:12.020
that I took as my
guides, and the first one

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:14.355
was that I wanted to make
my content accessible,

00:04:14.355 --> 00:04:16.730
and what that meant is that
people who should have access

00:04:16.730 --> 00:04:18.950
to the content, can get
it when they need it,

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:21.600
and somewhere that they
know where to find it.

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:23.060
The second thing
was reproducible.

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:28.060
Can I can I reliably understand
how the content exists?

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:29.060
Why it is the way it is?

00:04:29.060 --> 00:04:30.350
Can I sort of, audit it.

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:33.050
And then secure, and that's sort
of the inverse of accessible.

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:34.490
Should people who don't--

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:36.710
people who shouldn't have
access, don't have access.

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:40.530
That's pretty important.

00:04:40.530 --> 00:04:43.230
So the question is
how do I get there.

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:46.460
And this is honestly
not a trivial problem.

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:48.590
In particular, in between
those important train

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:51.533
steps with my data, I have
to store that data somewhere.

00:04:51.533 --> 00:04:53.700
And then, in between training
and serving the model,

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:56.570
I have to store that
model somewhere.

00:04:56.570 --> 00:04:59.050
And until recently this
wasn't a solved problem.

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:02.018
You know, I could put it, the
analysis data in a database,

00:05:02.018 --> 00:05:04.310
if I have one handy, if I
have write privileges to one.

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:05.540
That's fine.

00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:07.220
But if I don't,
or if I don't want

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:09.512
to put it in a database for
some reason, there kind of,

00:05:09.512 --> 00:05:10.830
was no good answer there.

00:05:10.830 --> 00:05:13.160
And similarly with modeling.

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:15.020
There was kind of
no good answer.

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:19.340
So I went I went back to Star
Wars, went back to you know,

00:05:19.340 --> 00:05:22.070
not working on this, because
that was that was easier.

00:05:22.070 --> 00:05:25.490
And of course Yoda came
to my came to my rescue,

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:29.820
yet again, saying, "when
hopeless the task seems,

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:31.400
come out with a
sweet new R Package.

00:05:31.400 --> 00:05:33.260
Someone will."

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:34.950
It's inevitable.

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:35.690
You know.

00:05:35.690 --> 00:05:36.920
And of course that was true.

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:39.378
The pins pack to the new package
by Javier Luraschi.

00:05:39.378 --> 00:05:42.200
He's here at RStudio, and
this is a super cool package.

00:05:42.200 --> 00:05:43.730
I'm super excited about it.

00:05:43.730 --> 00:05:46.160
Basically, what it lets
you do is take something,

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:48.597
an R object, put
it somewhere else,

00:05:48.597 --> 00:05:50.180
and then get it back
when you want it.

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:52.430
This is sort of the essence
of the pins package.

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:54.222
But look let's go into
a little more detail

00:05:54.222 --> 00:05:55.827
about exactly how it works.

00:05:55.827 --> 00:05:58.160
So I'm going to start off
with my R, my RStudio session,

00:05:58.160 --> 00:05:59.420
and I have an object
I want to save.

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:00.860
You can think like a data frame.

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:01.818
It can also be a model.

00:06:01.818 --> 00:06:04.820
It could be any sort
of S3 compliant object.

00:06:04.820 --> 00:06:06.960
And I have a board
also, and my board

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:08.210
is where I'm going to save it.

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:10.957
Pins currently supports a
bunch of different boards,

00:06:10.957 --> 00:06:13.290
so RStudio Connect Kaggle,
GitHub, Azure, Google Cloud

00:06:13.290 --> 00:06:15.140
Platform, S3, or a website.

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:17.540
Javier is constantly adding
new boards I will also add.

00:06:17.540 --> 00:06:19.957
So if you want something that
isn't here, put it on GitHub

00:06:19.957 --> 00:06:22.457
and I'll probably have it done
before the end of conference.

00:06:22.457 --> 00:06:23.540
Kind of incredible.

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:25.370
But that aside, it
supports a lot different

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:26.240
places that you put your data.

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:26.990
Great.

00:06:26.990 --> 00:06:29.180
So it's really easy
to use as well.

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:31.220
You put your data
onto the board,

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:34.400
with the pins::pin command,
and get it back with

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:35.998
the pins::pin_get command.

00:06:35.998 --> 00:06:37.790
Let me show you
how this works, just

00:06:37.790 --> 00:06:42.260
to be a little
more concrete here.

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:46.990
So the way this works is
that I've got my pins--

00:06:46.990 --> 00:06:48.490
Let me make this
bigger for you all.

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:53.480
Better, easy to see, right?

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:56.020
So I'm going to load
up the pins package.

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:58.020
I'm going to register my
board, in this case I'm

00:06:58.020 --> 00:06:59.400
registering RStudio Connect.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:02.190
This just means this is
a board you can talk to.

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:03.120
To the the R session.

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:06.360
So it's going to register,
takes just a moment.

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:09.080
And then I'm going to pin
my data to the board, right?

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:11.080
It's a pin, whatever the
object is I'm

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:13.990
pining, the name of the object
and the board where it's going.

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:16.150
And it's going.

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:17.470
Taking a moment to load.

00:07:17.470 --> 00:07:19.220
And so here I am in
the connections pane.

00:07:19.220 --> 00:07:21.387
You can see that, like any
other type of connection,

00:07:21.387 --> 00:07:23.520
like a database connection,
it loads up right here.

00:07:23.520 --> 00:07:24.490
And if what I pin
is a data frame,

00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:26.198
I can actually see
what's going on there.

00:07:26.198 --> 00:07:29.022
I can see the different
fields in my data frame,

00:07:29.022 --> 00:07:31.230
and I can also go over here,
I can preview the table.

00:07:31.230 --> 00:07:32.522
I can see the first 1,000 rows.

00:07:32.522 --> 00:07:34.850
This is mtcars, there are
only 32 but, you know.

00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:36.350
You could see up to 1,000.

00:07:36.350 --> 00:07:38.450
And then if I want
to get the pin back,

00:07:38.450 --> 00:07:41.580
I just run this pin_get command,
saving it to a local file,

00:07:41.580 --> 00:07:43.940
and now I've got
mtcars back.

00:07:43.940 --> 00:07:47.120
To me, this is so, so cool.

00:07:47.120 --> 00:07:49.940
Because what's happened
now is, instead

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:51.818
of having to email
around data, or having

00:07:51.818 --> 00:07:54.110
to save data somewhere then
tell everybody where it is.

00:07:54.110 --> 00:07:57.060
And you know, version 2 final,
actually final, Alex final,

00:07:57.060 --> 00:07:57.560
right?

00:07:57.560 --> 00:07:58.730
None of that.

00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:00.770
The person needs to know
the name of the board,

00:08:00.770 --> 00:08:02.390
or the URL of the
board, and they

00:08:02.390 --> 00:08:04.763
need to know what the
name of the pin is.

00:08:04.763 --> 00:08:06.680
And then what they get
when they do a pin get,

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:08.335
is always the newest version.

00:08:08.335 --> 00:08:09.960
This is a thing that's
really exciting,

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:13.190
it's like the newest version is
always available on this pin.

00:08:25.690 --> 00:08:28.220
So the question is sort of,
what are good things to pin,

00:08:28.220 --> 00:08:28.970
and what are bad things?

00:08:28.970 --> 00:08:31.510
Obviously, you don't want to
just like, store every single R

00:08:31.510 --> 00:08:34.580
object you've ever
used in a pin.

00:08:34.580 --> 00:08:38.620
So some, again,
wisdom from Yoda.

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:41.110
Just before Luke goes off to
fight Vader at Cloud City,

00:08:41.110 --> 00:08:41.919
and it's a disa--

00:08:41.919 --> 00:08:43.293
anyway.

00:08:43.293 --> 00:08:44.710
Before that though,
Yoda gives him

00:08:44.710 --> 00:08:46.750
some very, very sage advice.

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:49.840
"Best considered a cache-ing
mechanism, pins are."

00:08:49.840 --> 00:08:51.520
And what this means--

00:08:51.520 --> 00:08:53.890
A, it's kind of, this
movie came out in 1980,

00:08:53.890 --> 00:08:55.750
and this package
came out in 2020.

00:08:55.750 --> 00:08:57.940
So like, this is very
impressive to begin with.

00:08:57.940 --> 00:09:00.340
But even more impressive
is that he's right.

00:09:00.340 --> 00:09:04.720
And so you generally don't want
to save your data of record

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:05.560
in a pin.

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:07.250
It's not a database.

00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:09.250
It doesn't have all the
like, bells and whistles

00:09:09.250 --> 00:09:11.380
of a database,
being able to audit.

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:13.763
Not a database replacement.

00:09:13.763 --> 00:09:16.180
What pins are great for is,
is they're great for something

00:09:16.180 --> 00:09:17.710
that you could
compute at the time,

00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:20.140
or you could get at the
time, but it's easier

00:09:20.140 --> 00:09:21.160
to store it somewhere.

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:25.150
You'd rather cache it, rather
than recreating it on demand.

00:09:25.150 --> 00:09:26.760
And so there are
three attributes

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:28.260
that good candidates
for a pin have.

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:30.010
And that is that they're
relatively small,

00:09:30.010 --> 00:09:31.210
say like, a gigabyte.

00:09:31.210 --> 00:09:32.710
There's no hard
rule, but a gigabyte

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:34.720
is a good rule of thumb.

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:35.680
They're reused.

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:38.760
You actually want this thing
over, and over, and over again,

00:09:38.760 --> 00:09:40.510
in different pieces
of your content.

00:09:40.510 --> 00:09:42.070
And that they're current.

00:09:42.070 --> 00:09:43.270
You want the tip of the pin.

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:44.895
You only care about
the newest version.

00:09:44.895 --> 00:09:46.480
If you care about
archival versions,

00:09:46.480 --> 00:09:48.020
a pin is not a great choice.

00:09:48.020 --> 00:09:50.690
But if you care about just the
newest one, that's really good.

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:53.980
And again, pins are for
things you can recreate.

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:55.720
It's not for your
data of record,

00:09:55.720 --> 00:09:57.543
that that's not a great choice.

00:09:57.543 --> 00:09:59.960
I mean, if you do it, whatever,
I'm not going to stop you.

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:03.160
But officially
recommending against that.

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:04.130
So let me show you.

00:10:04.130 --> 00:10:07.232
Let's go back to our bike
app here, and just show

00:10:07.232 --> 00:10:09.190
a little bit of where
pins show up in this app.

00:10:09.190 --> 00:10:11.140
We're very zoomed in.

00:10:11.140 --> 00:10:13.530
And so here we have the app.

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:14.030
Reminder.

00:10:14.030 --> 00:10:14.740
This is what our app looks like.

00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:15.790
We've got a map, we
click on the map,

