00:12:16.830 --> 00:12:18.500
This is my modeling
parameters actually,

00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:20.357
that I'm writing elsewhere.

00:12:20.357 --> 00:12:22.190
But what I really want
to show is, down here

00:12:22.190 --> 00:12:26.090
I'm training my model
with parsnip xgb_train.

00:12:26.090 --> 00:12:28.760
And then what I'm saving
here is this list object.

00:12:28.760 --> 00:12:31.190
This is like, this could be a
formal S3 object-- it's not.

00:12:31.190 --> 00:12:32.450
It's just a list.

00:12:32.450 --> 00:12:33.283
But I have my model.

00:12:33.283 --> 00:12:34.992
I'm saving some metadata
with it as well.

00:12:34.992 --> 00:12:36.680
The training date,
the splitting date,

00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:38.510
between my testing
and training set.

00:12:38.510 --> 00:12:41.240
And the recipe that I'm using
from the recipes package,

00:12:41.240 --> 00:12:43.220
to actually make
my training data.

00:12:43.220 --> 00:12:46.240
And then I'm pinning that
here, onto RStudio Connect.

00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:47.330
And this is super useful.

00:12:47.330 --> 00:12:51.058
Now I have a really good
way to know what I've done.

00:12:51.058 --> 00:12:52.600
And then last, my
Plumber API, that's

00:12:52.600 --> 00:12:54.470
serving those predictions.

00:12:54.470 --> 00:12:58.160
I've got my model, I'm
reading it in here.

00:12:58.160 --> 00:12:59.990
And then all this
code basically,

00:12:59.990 --> 00:13:04.753
is just sort of interpreting
the input from the API,

00:13:04.753 --> 00:13:06.920
and making sure it's ready
to be fed into the model.

00:13:06.920 --> 00:13:08.378
And then down here,
and I'm running

00:13:08.378 --> 00:13:11.270
that predict command on that
model, that's from the pin.

00:13:11.270 --> 00:13:13.600
And this is you know, purring
away on RStudio Connect.

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:17.150
And so you know, I can come
here to the swagger interface,

00:13:17.150 --> 00:13:17.900
and get back.

00:13:17.900 --> 00:13:20.420
You know, these are the
predictions for station 75.

00:13:20.420 --> 00:13:21.860
I don't know what
station that is,

00:13:21.860 --> 00:13:23.750
but that's why I
have the other one.

00:13:23.750 --> 00:13:26.450
The other pin with
the station names.

00:13:26.450 --> 00:13:31.670
So that's how pins sort
of fit into this app.

00:13:31.670 --> 00:13:33.170
So now I've got a
solution for this.

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:36.170
For my analysis data, I can do
it in a database or in a pin,

00:13:36.170 --> 00:13:38.720
for my model I can
do it in a pin.

00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:41.095
One thing I do want to say
is, these are just slots,

00:13:41.095 --> 00:13:43.220
and you can put whatever
you want into these slots.

00:13:43.220 --> 00:13:46.520
So for example, if you're
using some model in Python,

00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:48.088
or some import method in Python.

00:13:48.088 --> 00:13:49.630
You can totally do
that, and use

00:13:49.630 --> 00:13:52.910
Reticulate to go back and forth
between R and Python super easily.

00:13:52.910 --> 00:13:56.090
If what you're serving is
an a model, if it's plots,

00:13:56.090 --> 00:13:59.000
or just some kind of
counting, the same principle

00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:00.620
totally works.

00:14:00.620 --> 00:14:02.932
And when you're sharing,
if what you've got

00:14:02.932 --> 00:14:05.390
is an R Markdown document, that
you want to render or email

00:14:05.390 --> 00:14:06.557
out, or something like that.

00:14:06.557 --> 00:14:09.040
Again, think of these as slots.

00:14:09.040 --> 00:14:10.668
My version happened
to be R Markdown--

00:14:10.668 --> 00:14:11.960
R Markdown, Plummer, Shiny app.

00:14:11.960 --> 00:14:15.000
But yours can be
whatever you want.

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:17.770
OK, so pins has really
helped me a lot,

00:14:17.770 --> 00:14:21.070
make this content more
accessible, more reproducible.

00:14:21.070 --> 00:14:23.710
But I haven't really
addressed security at all,

00:14:23.710 --> 00:14:26.580
and that's a really
important piece here,

00:14:26.580 --> 00:14:28.210
the security of my app.

00:14:28.210 --> 00:14:31.050
And so of course, I
went back to Yoda.

00:14:31.050 --> 00:14:32.800
In "Return of the
Jedi," as Yoda is dying,

00:14:32.800 --> 00:14:36.340
fading away after 800 years
of training Jedi knights,

00:14:36.340 --> 00:14:38.060
witnessing the
downfall-- anyway, OK.

00:14:38.060 --> 00:14:41.560
I'm sorry, I really
like Star Wars.

00:14:41.560 --> 00:14:43.690
He gives this one final
piece of advice to Luke,

00:14:43.690 --> 00:14:46.000
and he says, "deploy
to the right server,

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:48.340
and secure your data will be."

00:14:48.340 --> 00:14:51.680
Which you know, I think is
very, very prescient of him.

00:14:51.680 --> 00:14:53.920
And you can do this
in an open source way,

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:56.170
if you know that's what
you're more comfortable doing.

00:14:56.170 --> 00:14:57.960
For this import
and training step,

00:14:57.960 --> 00:15:00.740
you know, put it on a server
any server that has R works.

00:15:00.740 --> 00:15:03.287
You can use a Cron Job if
you want to schedule it.

00:15:03.287 --> 00:15:04.870
For your analysis
data and your model,

00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:06.670
you can use-- if you have
a database for the database

00:15:06.670 --> 00:15:07.830
stuff, or a pins board.

00:15:07.830 --> 00:15:09.880
I showed there are like, a
whole bunch of options.

00:15:09.880 --> 00:15:13.508
For serving the model, any
server with R, again, can work.

00:15:13.508 --> 00:15:15.550
You do have some things
to be delicate with here,

00:15:15.550 --> 00:15:17.302
around how auth works, right?

00:15:17.302 --> 00:15:19.010
So you can do that
with networking rules.

00:15:19.010 --> 00:15:20.950
You could put a
proxy in front of it.

00:15:20.950 --> 00:15:23.050
You can like, route it
through a lambda gateway,

00:15:23.050 --> 00:15:24.160
to take care of API keys.

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:25.660
You have lots of
options, but you're

00:15:25.660 --> 00:15:28.742
going to want to do
something to secure this API.

00:15:28.742 --> 00:15:30.700
And then for sharing you
have a lot of options.

00:15:30.700 --> 00:15:33.260
If it's an R Markdown document,
either a website or a rendered

00:15:33.260 --> 00:15:34.000
document.

00:15:34.000 --> 00:15:35.800
Netlify is a great choice.

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:38.320
Shiny Server open source
is available to you,

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:40.060
and shinyapps.io is
also an option, which

00:15:40.060 --> 00:15:43.403
is not open source, but is
relatively affordable, so that

00:15:43.403 --> 00:15:45.070
can be a really good
option here, if you

00:15:45.070 --> 00:15:47.050
doing like a private
thing, or something

00:15:47.050 --> 00:15:49.403
that's for your own use.

00:15:49.403 --> 00:15:51.070
Of course, we see a
lot of organizations

00:15:51.070 --> 00:15:53.020
that sort of struggle
with pieces of this.

00:15:53.020 --> 00:15:55.145
Either they don't want to
manage this many sort of,

00:15:55.145 --> 00:15:56.620
separate pieces.

00:15:56.620 --> 00:15:59.620
Their IT organizations don't
want to manage stuff in R,

00:15:59.620 --> 00:16:00.400
we see that a lot.

00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:01.750
That's totally fine.

00:16:01.750 --> 00:16:04.090
And so of course, sort
of, easy mode here.

00:16:04.090 --> 00:16:06.360
Is our professional
product RStudio Connect,

00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:08.570
and that takes care of a
lot of these pieces for you.

00:16:08.570 --> 00:16:11.230
It can deal with the API keys.

00:16:11.230 --> 00:16:13.750
It can deal with scheduling
the R Markdown documents.

00:16:13.750 --> 00:16:15.730
It can deal with securing
the Plumber APIs.

00:16:15.730 --> 00:16:17.680
It can deal with
accessibility controls

00:16:17.680 --> 00:16:19.513
and that kind of stuff,
and you can put pins

00:16:19.513 --> 00:16:20.510
on RStudio Connect.

00:16:20.510 --> 00:16:26.720
So this can be a really
useful way to do all of this.

00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:29.450
So big takeaways.

00:16:29.450 --> 00:16:32.810
When you are thinking
about deploying content,

00:16:32.810 --> 00:16:36.230
the top three things to
really optimize for are--

00:16:36.230 --> 00:16:39.632
accessibility,
reproducibility, and security.

00:16:39.632 --> 00:16:41.090
If you're hitting
these three keys,

00:16:41.090 --> 00:16:44.600
you're probably
increasing value with what

00:16:44.600 --> 00:16:48.110
you're doing, as opposed to
just adding sophistication.

00:16:48.110 --> 00:16:49.640
While you're doing
that, R Markdown,

00:16:49.640 --> 00:16:53.450
Shiny, Plumber and Pins,
are good friends of yours.

00:16:53.450 --> 00:16:55.410
And of course, may
the force be with you.

00:16:55.410 --> 00:16:56.706
Thanks everybody.

00:16:56.706 --> 00:16:58.658
[APPLAUSE]

