I failed to mention at
the top of things here,

00:17:12.300 --> 00:17:15.569
but if you have questions, we're
going to continue using Sli.do,

00:17:15.569 --> 00:17:16.670
as we did in the keynote.

00:17:16.670 --> 00:17:20.640
So we're in Grand Ballroom
B, so if you have a question,

00:17:20.640 --> 00:17:23.459
go to the same Sli.do page,
select Grand Ballroom B

00:17:23.459 --> 00:17:26.099
and you can enter your questions
there, or vote on questions

00:17:26.099 --> 00:17:27.475
that are already asked there.

00:17:27.475 --> 00:17:29.100
I'm going to ask
Alex some questions

00:17:29.100 --> 00:17:31.950
here, since we have
a couple minutes.

00:17:31.950 --> 00:17:34.690
Maybe this was covered in the
last half of your presentation,

00:17:34.690 --> 00:17:36.570
but do you want
to readdress, how

00:17:36.570 --> 00:17:39.630
pins is different from a well
organized shared file system,

00:17:39.630 --> 00:17:41.210
with correct ACLs?

00:17:41.210 --> 00:17:44.270
Yeah, that's a great question.

00:17:44.270 --> 00:17:46.770
To be honest, under the hood
it's not that different, right?

00:17:46.770 --> 00:17:49.920
You have got the same kind of,
control and access controls.

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:52.650
The main difference is that
it's a very R centric answer,

00:17:52.650 --> 00:17:55.472
and so you can, from within
R, address those objects,

00:17:55.472 --> 00:17:57.930
without having to go through
the hassle of figuring out how

00:17:57.930 --> 00:17:59.790
your shared file system works.

00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:01.440
Finding things and
that sort of thing.

00:18:01.440 --> 00:18:04.120
So from an IT perspective,
maybe not that different.

00:18:04.120 --> 00:18:06.000
But from the perspective
of an R developer,

00:18:06.000 --> 00:18:08.250
it's a very different sort
of, development experience.

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:10.870
It's much more straightforward.

00:18:10.870 --> 00:18:12.570
This next one, we get a lot.

00:18:12.570 --> 00:18:15.260
So I'm happy to ask
it here-- what's

00:18:15.260 --> 00:18:19.788
the benefit of using a Plumber
API for saving the object to S3

00:18:19.788 --> 00:18:21.330
and then calling it
with a Shiny app?

00:18:21.330 --> 00:18:22.497
Oh, that's a great question.

00:18:22.497 --> 00:18:24.780
I love that question.

00:18:24.780 --> 00:18:26.310
There are a couple of benefits.

00:18:26.310 --> 00:18:27.753
One is that you can--

00:18:27.753 --> 00:18:29.420
the biggest benefit
to me, and then this

00:18:29.420 --> 00:18:30.878
happened in several
different ways,

00:18:30.878 --> 00:18:34.947
is that it decouples the
logic of serving the model,

00:18:34.947 --> 00:18:37.280
from the logic of displaying
whatever you're displaying,

00:18:37.280 --> 00:18:38.613
or doing something else with it.

00:18:38.613 --> 00:18:40.430
So you decouple those
things, and that

00:18:40.430 --> 00:18:41.120
makes your life easier.

00:18:41.120 --> 00:18:42.540
It makes your life easier
in a couple of ways.

00:18:42.540 --> 00:18:44.708
One of them is purely
from a code perspective--

00:18:44.708 --> 00:18:46.250
it makes it easier
to write the code,

00:18:46.250 --> 00:18:48.470
because you write your Plumber
API to serve the model.

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:49.970
You write your Shiny
app, or whatever it is,

00:18:49.970 --> 00:18:51.803
to consume the model
and you get to sort of,

00:18:51.803 --> 00:18:53.720
separate those concerns,
which is really nice.

00:18:53.720 --> 00:18:55.720
The other piece from a
performance perspective--

00:18:55.720 --> 00:18:58.040
you can test those two
things independently.

00:18:58.040 --> 00:19:00.290
You can test the Plumber
API with say, the load test

00:19:00.290 --> 00:19:01.957
package, which I think
some people might

00:19:01.957 --> 00:19:03.402
be talking about next.

00:19:03.402 --> 00:19:05.360
And then you can test
the Shiny app separately.

00:19:05.360 --> 00:19:08.480
You can decouple both the coding
and the performance issues,

00:19:08.480 --> 00:19:14.050
between the API and the
app, which is great.

00:19:14.050 --> 00:19:15.280
OK, next.

00:19:15.280 --> 00:19:17.270
Is there a limit to
the amount of data

00:19:17.270 --> 00:19:18.820
you should be using with pins?

00:19:20.995 --> 00:19:22.620
It depends on which
board you're using.

00:19:22.620 --> 00:19:24.578
Some of those boards do
have limits to how much

00:19:24.578 --> 00:19:25.860
you can save.

00:19:25.860 --> 00:19:28.980
You know, my suggestion would
be that, if you're saving more

00:19:28.980 --> 00:19:31.440
than a gigabyte's worth of
data, there's probably a better

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:34.470
solution for what you're doing.

00:19:34.470 --> 00:19:38.310
And that's totally a rule
of thumb, not a real thing.

00:19:38.310 --> 00:19:40.950
But just in general, if you're
finding yourself doing more

00:19:40.950 --> 00:19:43.290
than that, you're
probably saving

00:19:43.290 --> 00:19:45.800
an object that's bigger than
pins really is designed for.

00:19:45.800 --> 00:19:48.060
One thing that
can be helpful is,

00:19:48.060 --> 00:19:50.685
if you're saving model objects,
often the model objects package

00:19:50.685 --> 00:19:53.143
up a lot of stuff with it, that
you don't necessarily want,

00:19:53.143 --> 00:19:54.350
like the training data.

00:19:54.350 --> 00:19:56.275
And that could make
your model really big,

00:19:56.275 --> 00:19:57.900
and you know, sort
of inflate the size.

00:19:57.900 --> 00:20:00.780
There is a package called
Butcher, like you know,

00:20:00.780 --> 00:20:05.040
person who makes meat cuts,
that you can use to sort of,

00:20:05.040 --> 00:20:06.720
chop out some of the
stuff in your model

00:20:06.720 --> 00:20:09.262
object you don't want, and just
save like actually the model.

00:20:09.262 --> 00:20:13.490
So if you're saving models, that
can be a useful thing to do.

00:20:13.490 --> 00:20:15.320
Let's do one more here, I
like this one as well.

00:20:15.320 --> 00:20:17.870
Is there a reason your
import and model training

00:20:17.870 --> 00:20:21.890
code is in R Markdown files,
instead of plain R scripts.

00:20:21.890 --> 00:20:23.130
Yes, that's a great question.

00:20:23.130 --> 00:20:24.275
I love it when things that
I put in the presentation

00:20:24.275 --> 00:20:26.420
and then took out, because
I didn't have time,

00:20:26.420 --> 00:20:28.280
come back in questions.

00:20:28.280 --> 00:20:31.830
Yeah, so I really love R
Markdown for two main reasons.

00:20:31.830 --> 00:20:34.070
One is that it encourages
literate programming that

00:20:34.070 --> 00:20:37.790
is interspersing code and
prose, because you have just

00:20:37.790 --> 00:20:39.090
like, a place to write prose.

00:20:39.090 --> 00:20:41.132
It doesn't have to be
comments, you just write it

00:20:41.132 --> 00:20:42.170
and it's great.

00:20:42.170 --> 00:20:43.870
The other reason, is that you
have a record of what you did.

00:20:43.870 --> 00:20:46.460
So when you come back later,
and something has gone awry

00:20:46.460 --> 00:20:48.920
with your model, you
have output there,

00:20:48.920 --> 00:20:50.600
with the code of what
actually happened.

00:20:50.600 --> 00:20:52.250
Unlike if it's just
an R script and then,

00:20:52.250 --> 00:20:53.930
whatever happened was
totally ephemeral,

00:20:53.930 --> 00:20:55.310
and it's gone forever.

00:20:58.100 --> 00:21:00.100
Everyone, please join me
in thanking Alex again.

00:21:00.100 --> 00:21:02.010
Thanks everybody.

00:21:02.010 --> 00:21:04.460
